WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.402
-Our next guest
is a Grammy-nominated,

00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:04.070
multi-platinum-selling comedian.

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:05.505
His special,
"The Good Old Days,"

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:07.974
is streaming globally
on Netflix now.

00:00:07.974 --> 00:00:09.809
Please welcome to the show
Jeff Foxworthy, everyone.

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:11.310
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:21.320 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:24.090 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:25.958
I'm so happy
to have you here, Jeff.

00:00:25.958 --> 00:00:27.193
-I'm thrilled to be here.

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:29.996
-You had a weekend
very different than mine.

00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:33.199
You went on
an arrowhead hunting trip,

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:34.667
which I didn't know
you could even do.

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:36.902
-Oh, I go all over the country.
-Okay.

00:00:36.902 --> 00:00:38.772
And are there hot spots
for arrowheads?

00:00:38.772 --> 00:00:40.339
-Yeah, there's --
Well, there's places like

00:00:40.339 --> 00:00:42.642
where they had better material.
-Okay.

00:00:42.642 --> 00:00:44.977
-'Cause I have a farm in
Georgia, but we have quartz.

00:00:44.977 --> 00:00:46.279
They're just not very pretty.

00:00:46.279 --> 00:00:48.414
And then,
I have friends in Arkansas

00:00:48.414 --> 00:00:50.082
that have this beautiful flint,

00:00:50.082 --> 00:00:53.219
and they just live in, like,
a hot spot, so we swap off.

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:56.589
They come to my farm and have
fun, and then I go to theirs.

00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:59.725
-And what's the process of going
about arrowhead hunting?

00:00:59.725 --> 00:01:02.061
I would imagine just looking
down at the ground a lot, right?

00:01:02.061 --> 00:01:04.497
-It's --
You would be good at it.

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:06.465
[ Laughter ]

00:01:06.465 --> 00:01:08.301
I mean, it's pretty much --
They had to live near

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:11.036
water sources,
so you're looking for creeks

00:01:11.036 --> 00:01:13.272
or rivers and like
an elevated flat spot.

00:01:13.272 --> 00:01:17.143
And it's -- like, I would
never dig, like, a grave.

00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:18.744
That's bad mojo.
-Yeah.

00:01:18.744 --> 00:01:23.015
-But you're finding, like, their
knives and their tools, their --

00:01:23.015 --> 00:01:24.784
that, you know --
and some of them --

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:27.754
Like, I've found one this
weekend that's 10,000 years old.

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:29.421
-That's incredible.
-Perfect shape.

00:01:29.421 --> 00:01:32.658
-And so you have a farm
in Georgia, you mentioned --

00:01:32.658 --> 00:01:34.694
3,000 acres.
Do you find -- are there things

00:01:34.694 --> 00:01:37.864
you unearth there on your own
personal farm?

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:40.233
-Not like bodies or anything,
but...

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:42.302
[ Laughter ]
Yeah, like I have --

00:01:42.302 --> 00:01:44.037
We plant about 300 or 400 acres,

00:01:44.037 --> 00:01:46.539
and then I have
a blueberry orchard.

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:48.808
I have a greenhouse.
I have a big garden.

00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:51.444
I have -- I started --
When COVID hit,

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:54.614
my daughter bought me a beehive,
and I was like scared to death,

00:01:54.614 --> 00:01:56.882
but we started raising bees.

00:01:56.882 --> 00:01:58.484
-Really?
-And doing honey.

00:01:58.484 --> 00:02:00.253
-And how's your honey?
Is it a tasty honey?

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:01.887
-Oh, my honey's hot,
let me tell you.

00:02:01.887 --> 00:02:03.623
[ Laughter ]
Yeah.

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:04.824
-I feel like
I keep walking into these.

00:02:04.824 --> 00:02:06.993
[ Laughter ]

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:08.694
-It's good.

00:02:08.694 --> 00:02:10.496
-You know,
you live in Georgia now,

00:02:10.496 --> 00:02:13.132
and obviously you spent
a big chunk of your career

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:15.134
out in L.A.
-Mm-hmm.

00:02:15.134 --> 00:02:17.236
-Being a country boy,
being from Georgia,

00:02:17.236 --> 00:02:19.538
like, what was it like
in your years in L.A.?

00:02:19.538 --> 00:02:23.375
-Oh, I didn't --
I didn't blend in very well.

00:02:23.375 --> 00:02:26.712
When I had a sitcom, we used
to film next to "Seinfeld."

00:02:26.712 --> 00:02:30.082
-Yeah.
-And I --

00:02:30.082 --> 00:02:31.918
When Ram first came out
with that truck, I thought,

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:33.186
"Well,
that's a cool-looking truck,"

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:35.454
and I actually went
to Beverly Hills Dodge and said,

00:02:35.454 --> 00:02:38.824
"I want to buy a truck,"
and they were like, "To drive?"

00:02:38.824 --> 00:02:41.027
I'm like,
"Yeah, I want a truck."

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:43.262
And so I would pull in to work
in my truck,

00:02:43.262 --> 00:02:45.064
and Seinfeld
would come pulling in

00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:46.866
in one of his 50 Porsches next.

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:49.368
He's like, "Zzzt!
Good morning, loser."

00:02:49.368 --> 00:02:51.904
You know, it was...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:51.904 --> 00:02:53.506
So, yeah.
-Yeah. Well, you stuck by --

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.141
You stuck by
what was true for you.

00:02:55.141 --> 00:02:57.343
-I did. I did.
I stuck to my roots.

00:02:57.343 --> 00:03:00.312
-So, 40 -- you've been doing
comedy for 40 years, and I'm --

00:03:00.312 --> 00:03:01.948
I haven't been doing it that
long, but I'm still --

00:03:01.948 --> 00:03:04.650
I have been doing it a while,
and one of the funniest things

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.787
to me is when you can
still be wrong about a joke

00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:09.055
until you do it --

00:03:09.055 --> 00:03:10.689
you find out when you do it
in front of an audience.

00:03:10.689 --> 00:03:12.258
Do you still find that
40 years in?

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:13.893
-Yeah, I --

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:15.394
'Cause I've done it
in every form.

00:03:15.394 --> 00:03:17.997
I've done page-a-day calendars
for 30-something years.

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:20.099
I've written 20-something books.

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:25.137
Stand-up, the thing that
fascinates me about it is...

00:03:25.137 --> 00:03:27.539
if you laid carpet for 30 years,
you would know,

00:03:27.539 --> 00:03:30.442
"Hey, when I get to the corner,
this is what I do."

00:03:30.442 --> 00:03:32.045
After 30,
I still don't know

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:34.313
what people are gonna laugh at.
-I know.

00:03:34.313 --> 00:03:36.482
-Doesn't that amaze you?
-It's fascinating, yeah.

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:38.952
-And so I don't want to go,
like, on a Saturday night

00:03:38.952 --> 00:03:40.252
'cause they laugh at everything.

00:03:40.252 --> 00:03:41.720
I want to go, like,
on a Monday or Tuesday night,

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.222
30 people there.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:43.222 --> 00:03:45.024
And I take boxes.

00:03:45.024 --> 00:03:46.960
I take three boxes, and it's --

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.097
they're labeled gold, silver,
and "Certificate of Appearance."

00:03:51.097 --> 00:03:52.398
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:55.201
And I just have these --
these cards with these ideas.

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:57.503
But if you and I
were walking in, and you said,

00:03:57.503 --> 00:03:59.939
"Pick the four
that are gonna work the best,"

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:02.375
I would still be dead wrong
on two of them.

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:04.877
-I bet that is a great --
By doing that

00:04:04.877 --> 00:04:06.345
and by giving the audience
that power,

00:04:06.345 --> 00:04:08.314
I bet they take it
more seriously and --

00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:10.816
-Oh, they're totally
invested in it, yeah.

00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:13.987
And I'm like --
you know, like something --

00:04:13.987 --> 00:04:16.288
And I can always tell,
the terrible ones

00:04:16.288 --> 00:04:17.657
go in
"Certificate of Appearance,"

00:04:17.657 --> 00:04:19.291
and I always know
'cause the only person

00:04:19.291 --> 00:04:21.427
I hear laughing is my wife
in the back of the room.

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:22.462
[ Laughter ]

00:04:22.462 --> 00:04:23.996
But then
I try to argue with them.

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:26.799
I'm like, "Well, did I maybe
not set it up well?

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:28.934
Did you not understand
what I was saying?"

00:04:28.934 --> 00:04:30.236
And they're like,
"No, it's not funny.

00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:31.703
Put it in
the "Certificate of Appearance."

00:04:31.703 --> 00:04:33.072
-Yeah, there's nothing worse
than when they're like,

00:04:33.072 --> 00:04:35.208
"No, we get it."
-Yeah, "It's not funny."

00:04:35.208 --> 00:04:36.642
-"We hate it."
-Yeah.

00:04:36.642 --> 00:04:37.877
-Now, your wife
laughing in the back,

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:39.479
is that because
she's your number-one fan

00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:41.647
or she enjoys watching you bomb?

00:04:41.647 --> 00:04:43.082
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:44.917
-Loves watching me bomb.
-Loves watching you --

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:47.319
-Loves watching me bomb.
Like, she'll get in the car.

00:04:47.319 --> 00:04:49.788
She goes, "That thing about your
grandmother, nobody laughed!

00:04:49.788 --> 00:04:51.890
There was no..."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:55.794
"I told you that wasn't funny,
but you wouldn't listen to me!"

00:04:55.794 --> 00:04:57.930
-It's so funny.
I -- you know,

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.600
I don't know what your wife's
background is professionally,

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.568
but my wife
has nothing to do with comedy.

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:05.238
And so there are times where
she will say the same thing,

00:05:05.238 --> 00:05:07.974
"That's not gonna work,"
and I do kind of, you know,

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:10.008
impress upon her, like, "Look,
I am the one who does this."

00:05:10.008 --> 00:05:12.244
-"I'm the expert."
-And then I'll come home.

00:05:12.244 --> 00:05:13.979
-I bet she's always right.
-And I'll come home,

00:05:13.979 --> 00:05:15.714
and I will -- you know,
I'll have an audio recording

00:05:15.714 --> 00:05:17.417
of the show, and she'll go,
"How'd that joke go?"

00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:19.285
and I'm like, "Not bad," and
she's like, "Let me hear it."

00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:21.453
I'm like, "All right."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.453 --> 00:05:22.522
"It ate it.
It ate it hard."

00:05:22.522 --> 00:05:23.922
-All right.
So let me ask you this.

00:05:23.922 --> 00:05:26.793
Where do you come up
with most material?

00:05:26.793 --> 00:05:29.562
-I think just
over the course of the day.

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:31.564
I think --
Usually, I just have to be alone

00:05:31.564 --> 00:05:33.733
and have to be quiet.
-Yeah, you need quiet.

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:35.635
-Yeah.
-Like, and maybe this is why.

00:05:35.635 --> 00:05:39.004
I bet I think of 80%
of the stuff in the shower.

00:05:39.004 --> 00:05:40.840
-Yeah, it does quiet it down.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:45.845
-And I have a pad like on my
bathroom counter where I write.

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:48.448
But I have spent
the last 37 years

00:05:48.448 --> 00:05:51.650
coming out of the shower
and saying to my wife,

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:54.253
"Is this funny?"
[ Laughter ]

00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:57.156
And it's when she says yes
and I haven't even

00:05:57.156 --> 00:05:59.225
said anything yet,
that's when it...

00:05:59.225 --> 00:06:01.194
[ Laughter ]

00:06:01.194 --> 00:06:02.561
But she has great instincts.

00:06:02.561 --> 00:06:04.564
I mean, she'll go, "That's --
No, that's not funny."

00:06:04.564 --> 00:06:06.064
-Yeah.
And then, you know,

00:06:06.064 --> 00:06:08.300
that's the part about it
that is so valuable

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.270
is they're closer to what
matters, which is an audience.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:12.571
-Yeah.
-Whereas...

00:06:12.571 --> 00:06:14.306
-Well, and I don't know,
but I always assume

00:06:14.306 --> 00:06:15.508
the audience is right.

00:06:15.508 --> 00:06:17.276
If they say it's not funny...
-Yes, I agree.

00:06:17.276 --> 00:06:19.978
-You know, and sometimes you'll
throw something out you think,

00:06:19.978 --> 00:06:23.415
"This is stupid," and then
people snot on themselves,

00:06:23.415 --> 00:06:27.753
and you're like, "Really?
Really? That's funny?"

00:06:27.753 --> 00:06:30.890
So I guess stand-up is --
that's why it's fascinating.

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:34.960
It's like -- to me, it's like
the woman I can't figure out,

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:36.194
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:06:36.194 --> 00:06:37.897
-Which makes her
really interesting

00:06:37.897 --> 00:06:39.598
because I can never
quite figure her out.

00:06:39.598 --> 00:06:41.334
-I think that is --
that's a good way of saying it.

00:06:41.334 --> 00:06:44.069
You talk about the --
This is --

00:06:44.069 --> 00:06:46.171
You've talked obviously a lot
about the process of writing,

00:06:46.171 --> 00:06:48.040
but, also,
there's the performance of it.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:51.811
And you talk about one of the
dangers of having a big meal

00:06:51.811 --> 00:06:53.445
before a show,
and I'm assuming

00:06:53.445 --> 00:06:56.415
this is something that
you learned from experience.

00:06:56.415 --> 00:06:58.751
-Well, yes.
Well, like, for me,

00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:00.820
I feel like
it makes you sluggish,

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:03.389
so I never eat before a show.
I'm always hungry.

00:07:03.389 --> 00:07:06.558
But I did a lot of things
with Larry the Cable Guy.

00:07:06.558 --> 00:07:11.464
And so, we're up in Chicago,
and I sit --

00:07:11.464 --> 00:07:14.166
And we were doing a show
together in a theater,

00:07:14.166 --> 00:07:16.102
and I said, "Have you ever had
Chicago pizza?"

00:07:16.102 --> 00:07:18.304
"I don't like it.
I only like New York pizza."

00:07:18.304 --> 00:07:19.805
And I said,
"Well, have you ever tried it?"

00:07:19.805 --> 00:07:21.073
"No, never tried it."

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:23.242
I said, "Well, dude,
you got to try it before..."

00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:25.011
So I get my phone.

00:07:25.011 --> 00:07:26.178
There's a Lou Malnati's,
you know?

00:07:26.178 --> 00:07:29.482
-Yeah, the best.
-Love Lou -- love Lou's.

00:07:29.482 --> 00:07:32.618
"But I love New York pizza!"
So I call Lou Malnati's,

00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:35.021
and they actually deliver it
to the theater door.

00:07:35.021 --> 00:07:38.124
Well, anybody -- this thing's
like eating a slice of pie.

00:07:38.124 --> 00:07:40.159
-Yeah.
-I mean, it's like that thick.

00:07:40.159 --> 00:07:43.495
-It's like a pizza high-rise.
-Yeah, it's a pizza high-rise.

00:07:43.495 --> 00:07:46.699
So you can -- I mean, even if
I'm hungry, I can only eat one.

00:07:46.699 --> 00:07:49.902
-Yeah.
-So, I cut Larry a slice.

00:07:49.902 --> 00:07:52.037
I said, "Here, just try it."

00:07:52.037 --> 00:07:53.873
"That's pretty good
right there."

00:07:53.873 --> 00:07:57.643
He ate three quarters
of the pizza,

00:07:57.643 --> 00:07:59.412
and then he went on stage --

00:07:59.412 --> 00:08:01.814
"Jeff made me eat this pizza,
and I'm..."

00:08:01.814 --> 00:08:04.683
[ Laughter ]

00:08:04.683 --> 00:08:06.852
Burping into the microphone,
and...

00:08:06.852 --> 00:08:07.919
[ Laughter ]

00:08:07.919 --> 00:08:09.154
-Well, a cautionary tale,

00:08:09.154 --> 00:08:10.389
A cautionary tale.
-Yes.

00:08:10.389 --> 00:08:12.090
-Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:15.628
Congratulations on the special
and the entire career.

00:08:15.628 --> 00:08:18.097
It's an honor.
You guys, Jeff Foxworthy.

00:08:18.097 --> 00:08:21.033
"The Good Old Days" is streaming
globally on Netflix now.

